{% import 'chrome/macros.html.j2' as c %}
<section>
  <h2>Conclusion</h2>

  <p class="lede">Bucketer can give every browser real progress and real completion. It cannot
  give every browser the same destination or the same size ceiling, and no amount of engineering
  changes that. The recommendation is to ship three mechanisms, choose between them by asking the
  browser what it supports, and say plainly which one is in use.</p>

  <h3>Everything, in one table</h3>

  <p>Desktop capability rows are measured. Mobile rows and Safari's private-storage row are from
  documentation, not measurement.</p>

  <table class="matrix">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Capability</th><th>Chromium desktop</th><th>Chromium Android</th>
        <th>Firefox desktop</th><th>Firefox Android</th>
        <th>Safari desktop</th><th>Safari iOS</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tr>
      <td>Write to a chosen folder</td>
      <td>yes</td><td>yes, defects open</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Private storage (flat cost)</td>
      <td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td>
      <td>yes, from Safari 26</td><td>yes, from Safari 26</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Real progress and completion</td>
      <td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Resume after closing the tab</td>
      <td>yes, exact byte</td><td>unreliable</td><td>yes</td><td>unreliable</td>
      <td>7-day expiry</td><td>7-day expiry</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Folder structure preserved</td>
      <td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>no, flat</td><td>no, flat</td><td>no, flat</td><td>no, flat</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Background transfer service</td>
      <td>yes, unused</td><td>yes, unused</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Size, and what actually sets the limit</h3>

  <table class="matrix">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Target</th><th>Practical ceiling</th><th>What sets it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Chromium desktop</strong></td>
      <td>free disk</td>
      <td>Nothing else. Writes stream into the chosen folder at constant memory cost.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Firefox desktop</strong></td>
      <td>about 10 GB, or free disk with a granted allowance</td>
      <td>The default storage allowance, not memory. Staging also needs twice the file's
          space transiently.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Safari desktop</strong></td>
      <td>about 77 GB, unverified</td>
      <td>A generous storage allowance. The real limit is whether the export step behaves as
          it does elsewhere, which nobody has measured.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Chromium Android</strong></td>
      <td>low single-digit GB</td>
      <td>Not storage — the tab being suspended when the user leaves the browser.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Firefox Android</strong></td>
      <td>hundreds of MB</td>
      <td>The same suspension problem, with less headroom.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Safari iOS</strong></td>
      <td>about 100 MB</td>
      <td>Page memory. The page is killed with no error the application can catch, and
          JavaScript stops the moment the user switches apps.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Any browser, handoff mode</strong></td>
      <td>no limit</td>
      <td>Nothing is held by the application at all. This is why the least capable mechanism
          remains the only one for the largest jobs.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>What to do</h3>

  <table class="num">
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Decision</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead>
    <tr>
      <td>1</td>
      <td><strong>Use private origin storage for in-application downloads.</strong></td>
      <td>Measured flat at 22-92 MiB on Chromium and 15-25 MiB on Firefox from 64 MiB to 2 GiB.
          Holding the file in memory instead costs 1.00x its size on Chromium and 1.62x on
          Firefox, which caps the feature at the machine's memory.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2</td>
      <td><strong>Keep the browser-handoff path for the largest jobs.</strong></td>
      <td>It is the only mechanism with no ceiling, and it already works everywhere.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3</td>
      <td><strong>Keep the single-file architecture.</strong></td>
      <td>The capability it forgoes helps only the platform that already has a better option,
          and its main alternative has an open defect that reports success on a truncated
          file.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>4</td>
      <td><strong>Treat Safari as unverified, not unsupported.</strong></td>
      <td>The mechanism is documented there and the allowance is generous, but the step the
          design depends on has never been measured by anyone. Ship it behind detection and
          confirm before claiming it.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>5</td>
      <td><strong>Do not attempt large downloads on iPhone.</strong></td>
      <td>Roughly 100 MB, killed without warning, with JavaScript suspended the moment the
          user leaves the browser. Say so rather than fail silently.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  {{ c.note('The one open product question',
     'How long may a paused download sit before someone returns to it? Safari deletes site data
      after seven days without a visit. If sessions are hours or days apart this is a footnote;
      if a job may be abandoned for a month it disqualifies Safari from the staged mechanism
      entirely. This report does not answer it, because it is a decision about the product
      rather than a fact about browsers.') }}

  <h3>Next steps, in order</h3>

  <p>Two device checks and one decision close this out. On real Safari, measure whether the
  export step costs what it costs elsewhere — that single number decides the Safari tier. On a
  real Android device, confirm whether the folder picker and save dialog defects persist. And
  settle the resume window above. Further desk research has reached diminishing returns: the
  remaining gaps are gaps in what has been published, not in what has been read.</p>
</section>
